rules_version = '2';

// Deny everything, and that is the correct rule rather than a placeholder.
//
// Security Rules do not apply to server client libraries AT ALL: the Python client in
// disruptions/api/partner_store.py authenticates with the runtime service account and is
// authorized by project IAM (roles/datastore.user, granted in .entur/application.yaml), which
// Rules cannot narrow. So there is nothing here for the API to be granted.
//
// What this file DOES do is close the other door. A Firestore database in a project that has ever
// had a Firebase web/mobile app registered is reachable by an untrusted client SDK with an API key,
// and the default template ("allow read, write: if request.time < <a date>") opens it to the world
// until that date passes. These four collections hold partner subscriptions, callback destinations
// and the Norwegian prose of a traveller's disruption alert; none of it may be read by a browser.
//
// THIS FILE IS DEPLOYED, not only committed (2026-09-09). terraform/firestore.tf reads it into a
// google_firebaserules_ruleset and releases that as `cloud.firestore/<database>` for the named
// database, so a console edit towards the template above is drift the next apply reverts. The
// emulator reads the same file through firebase.json, and .github/workflows/terraform.yml lists it
// in both `paths:` lists so an edit here plans and applies like one to the indexes. Before that
// date the file asserted a protection nothing deployed -- true only because no Firebase app has
// ever been registered in these projects, which is exactly the state the release exists to survive.
//
// Note the partner receiver's OWN rules are a separate file in integrations/firebase-notifications/
// and live in the PARTNER's project, not this one.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
